// R11 partial-move residual: a record with one owning field moved out
// (`let owned moved: String = p.a`) was skipped whole at scope end, because
// codegen's drop admission read borrowck's binding-level `wasMoved`, so the
// record's SECOND owning field, `p.b`, was released by nothing. It never
// double freed: `moved` releases `a`, and nothing released `b`.
//
// CLOSED 2026-09-16: borrowck records each move WITH its field path
// (`Checker.moved_paths`), and codegen releases exactly the unmoved owning
// fields of a partially moved record (`emitPartialRecordDrop`). Measured
// 1000 before on both witnesses, through this same host and counter, on the
// tree before the change. It stays in the gate BECAUSE it is closed: pinned
// at 0, any nonzero reading re-opens it.
//
// The record is a `while`-body local on purpose. R11 row 4's block-scoped
// release (CLOSED 2026-09-15) is what gives a loop-body local a drop point at
// all, so this fixture exercises the partial drop through the same path a
// real loop takes.
//
// What this fixture does NOT cover, stated so nobody reads it as a proof:
// a field written again after its move (`p.a = "c"`), whose NEW value still
// leaks because the move record is never revived (R16 at field granularity,
// measured and described in docs/OWNERSHIP.md), and a CONDITIONAL partial
// move. Borrowck records a move made on one branch of
// an `if` as if it happened on every path, so the moved field is left
// unreleased on the path that did not move it. That is a leak by design
// (the fail-toward-leak asymmetry codegen.zig's header commits to), not a
// double free, and closing it needs runtime drop flags.
//
// Status: parses, passes `cell check`. LLVM and MLIR refuse it (`String`
// crossing the C boundary is scalar-first-refused there); lives under
// examples/leaks/ so it is invisible to the top-level corpus, agreement and
// mlir-lowering loops (see examples/leaks/README.md).

pub struct Pair {
    owned a: String
    owned b: String
}

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

pub fn main() {
    var i = 0
    while i < 1000 {
        let owned p: Pair = Pair { a: "aaaaaaaa", b: "bbbbbbbb" }
        let owned moved: String = p.a
        i = i + 1
    }
    print_int(i)
}
